[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127172,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipoma is a benign mesenchymal tumour composed of mature adipocytes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127173,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8850/0 Lipoma</p>\r\n<p>8862/0 Osteolipoma</p>\r\n<p>8862/0 Chondrolipoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127174,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E80.0Z Lipoma, unspecified site</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127175,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127176,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Osteolipoma; chondrolipoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127177,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Superficial lipomas usually occur in the subcutaneous tissue of the neck, upper trunk, and proximal limbs. Facial lesions usually only occur in a subfascial location on the forehead. Distal extremity involvement is infrequent.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127178,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipomas present as a slow-growing, mobile and painless mass. Multifocality may be sporadic or syndromic. In familial multiple lipomatosis, subcutaneous lipomas develop, usually in the third decade, on the forearms, trunk and thigh {3745530}. Multiple lipomas may also be seen in Proteus syndrome and Bannayan&ndash;Zonana syndrome.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127179,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipoma is the most common mesenchymal neoplasm in adults, typically presenting in the fifth or sixth decade, and is slightly more common in males. The diagnosis is uncommon in children.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127180,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority of lipomas are idiopathic. Familial multiple lipomatosis is usually autosomal dominant and the involved gene has been mapped to 12q14.3. Germline mutations in the <em>PTEN</em> tumour suppressor gene are common in patients with Proteus syndrome and Bannayan&ndash;Zonana syndrome.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127181,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The protein encoded by <em>HMGA2</em> contains structural DNA-binding domains that play an important role in embryological adipogenesis {26966068; 15593017}. Structural aberrations resulting in deregulation of the <em>HMGA2</em> gene are frequent in lipomas, with the most common being translocations involving 12q13-q15 {7606786; 23508853}. Rearrangements involving 6p21 (<em>HMGA1</em>) and interstitial deletions of 13q have also been reported {1988102; 11576996}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127182,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipomas are well-circumscribed, with a uniform glistening, yellow to cream cut surface. Some cases show macroscopic bone and/or cartilage.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127183,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipomas display a thin capsule and are composed of lobules of mature adipocytes, dissected by thin, incomplete fibrous septa. Additional features include fat necrosis with foamy macrophages, fibrosis and myxoid change. In some lesions, metaplastic bone and/or cartilage may be seen {21822651}. Adipocytes sometimes show intranuclear inclusions (Lochkern cells).</p>\r\n<h6>Immunohistochemistry</h6>\r\n<p>Lesional adipocytes are positive for HMGA2, which helps the distinction with normal adipose tissue {20834238}. However immunohistochemistry is rarely necessary for the diagnosis.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>Distinction from atypical lipomatous tumour (ALT) is based on the presence of nuclear enlargement and hyperchromatism, and stromal atypical cells in the latter.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127184,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127185,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular studies are usually not necessary for diagnosis. In challenging cases, studies for <em>MDM2</em> amplification may be helpful to exclude ALT {34997060}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127186,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> lobular proliferation of mature adipocytes; absence of cytological atypia (different from degenerate changes).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127187,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127188,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 224,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipomas are benign but may rarely recur (in &lt;&nbsp;5% of cases) in a non-destructive fashion.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-02-27T13:44:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]